                NEW ENGLAND ELECTRIC SELECTS DIGITAL
                  AS MAINFRAME ALTERNATIVE SUPPLIER


           ...Mainframe downsizing savings and innovative
           financing drive investments in Open Systems...


MAYNARD, Mass. -- December 12, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced that it has been selected by New England Electric's 
service company, New England Power Service Company (NEPSCo), to 
provide a range of Open Systems solutions over the next several 
years. The value of the first year of this multi-year contract is 
$2.2 million. New England Electric's retail subsidiaries serve 1.3 
million customers in Massachusetts, Rhode Island and New Hampshire. 

     Under the terms of the contract, Digital will provide NEPSCo 
Alpha systems running the OpenVMS and DEC OSF/1 UNIX operating 
systems, together with consulting services in order to update 
current systems, migrate from mainframe-based systems, and develop 
new solutions. The applications to be moved off the mainframe 
potentially touch every part of the company, from financial and 
human resources, to customer services and purchasing. 

     In an innovative leasing arrangement, Digital Financial 
Services (DFS), a division of GE Capital, developed a transition 
financing program that provided a mainframe upgrade to meet current 
computing needs. At the same time, DFS and Digital worked together 
to create a multi-year technology insertion plan to migrate the 
mainframe applications to an Alpha Open Systems environment. 

     According to Daniel J. Reddy, Manager of Technology Services at 
NEPSCo, the greatest appeal of the Digital solution is that 
projected savings in mainframe computing costs will fund the new 
technology infrastructure that the company needs as it aggressively 
pursues its information strategy. "We believe this new economic 
strategy offers many of the same benefits as outsourcing the 
mainframe, but without any loss of control," he said. 

     The service company recently implemented a suite of financial 
applications, which required an upgrade to the existing mainframe. 
"However," said Reddy, "We did not want to be tied into a 
traditional long-term contractual agreement that would hinder our 
continuing movement away from a mainframe-centric computing 
environment." 

     Reddy said that NEPSCo has already standardized on Alpha 
systems for its Information Technology needs between the desktop and 
the mainframe. Now the company is partnering with Digital on a plan 
to enable NEPSCo to move off mainframes over time to a 
less-expensive, more flexible Open Systems computing strategy. 

     Digital and DFS offered NEPSCo a unique, cost-saving agreement 
that for the first time meets the service company's need for 
flexible financing and mainframe migration. 

     "In conjunction with DFS, we are providing both financing to 
upgrade the mainframe and services to migrate mainframe applications 
to the 64-bit Alpha platform," said Kathy Power, Digital 
Northeastern Region Vice President. "Business practices like this 
offer our customers an easy path for managing their need to migrate 
from costly mainframes to today's Open Systems solutions, while 
providing for legacy mainframe applications as the migration 
proceeds."

     Jeff Amsler, General Manager of DFS, said, "Tailoring 
innovative financial solutions that enable Digital's customers to 
preserve capital resources while deploying current technology is 
what we do best.  With this opportunity, we answered an additional 
challenge -- to provide technology migration paths for NEPSCo."

     At more than a million dollars each, mainframe upgrades 
continue to be expensive. "As the utility industry enters a new era 
of rapidly changing competition, NEPSCo needs an Information 
Technology solution that provides incentives to invest in 
alternatives to costly mainframes," Reddy said. 

     "At the same time, we need to demonstrate to our customers and 
regulators that we are practicing prudent cost management. With 
Digital as an Information Technology partner, we have all these 
benefits from a vendor that clearly understands our Information 
Technology and business needs," he added.

     The first stage of this project will be for Digital to 
implement a Data Warehouse project. Subsequent stages include 
migration of specific mainframe applications as well as 
architectural planning and design. In addition, existing Digital 
VAX and ULTRIX platforms will be upgraded to Alpha systems. 

     New England Electric System is a public utility holding company 
with headquarters in Westborough, Massachusetts. Subsidiaries 
include retail electric companies, wholesale electric generating and 
transmission companies, and an oil and gas exploration and 
development company. NEPSCo provides support services to the other 
subsidiaries.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions, from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace. 
