         LIANT SHIPS C++/VIEWS FOR DIGITAL ALPHA PLATFORMS


             New Capabilities for DEC OSF/1 Developers


MAYNARD, Mass. -- December 14, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
and Liant Software today announced the availability of Liant's 
C++/Views on Digital's Alpha systems running the DEC OSF/1 operating 
system, Digital's award winning implementation of UNIX.  C++/Views 
is a tool for developing multi-platform, native graphical user 
interface (GUI) applications in C++.  It allows portable GUI 
applications to be rapidly developed once, and readily deployed 
without code modification, across a multitude of GUI platforms. 
Digital's next-generation family of AlphaServer and AlphaStation 
systems provides a high-performance open platform designed to 
improve the price/performance of client/server computing.

     "The advanced capabilities of C++/Views are an ideal match for 
Alpha systems," said Tim Yeaton, Digital's UNIX Group Manager. "The 
multi- platform features available in C++/Views eliminate much of 
the duplicate effort involved in creating open client/server 
solutions.  Combined with the outstanding performance of 
next-generation Alpha systems, C++/Views gives DEC OSF/1 developers 
a faster way to move client/server applications from development to 
deployment."

     With C++/Views, developers can create one set of source code 
and resources for their application, regardless of the number of 
platforms on which they want it to run.  When compiled on each 
platform, the application adheres to the native look and feel of 
that platform.

     C++/Views further speeds development using The Geometry 
Manager, a mechanism for automatically managing the size and 
position of interface components within dialogs.  Also included is 
a unique visual development tool, C++/Views Constructor, which 
provides the facility to draw and test interfaces within the same 
environment.  Both of these innovative tools are supported by a 
comprehensive library of more than 100 C++ classes, 

     "Our internal developers found that C++/Views running on an a 
DEC 3000 workstation cut their code compilation time by as much as 
half," said Joe DiSantis, general manager of Liant's Object 
Technology Division.  "We've seen first-hand that the Alpha platform 
provides the performance and scalability customers want for 
client/server application development."

     Digital's Alpha family was updated in November with a complete 
next-generation line of workstations and servers.  Three new 
workstations and five new servers offer high-speed Alpha chips in 
systems based on the common PCI bus, which lowers system costs while 
providing unparalleled openness and flexibility  As a result, the 
price/performance improvements of Alpha's next generation deliver a 
20 to 80 percent performance advantage at every price point.  
Digital's customers can benefit from high workstation performance 
levels that have not previously been available at the $10,000 to 
$16,000 range.

     C++/Views for DEC OSF/1 is available now and is priced at 
$2499.

     Liant Software Corporation, an approved Digital Business 
Partner, is a leading provider of tools and technologies that help 
software developers transition legacy data and applications to open 
client/server systems.  The company's comprehensive  portfolio of 
software development tools allows application portability across a 
variety of client/server hardware platforms and operating systems.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
