                  DIGITAL ANNOUNCES ACMSxp V2.0 --
                       FOR BUSINESS-CRITICAL,
           CLIENT/SERVER TP APPLICATIONS ON UNIX PLATFORMS


 ...support by Encina toolkit makes distributed transactions reliable


MAYNARD, Mass. -- December 19, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced version 2.0 of ACMSxp -- the first production-ready 
release of its open, portable transaction processing (TP) monitor 
software.  ACMSxp V2.0 runs on Alpha platforms using the DEC OSF/1 
UNIX operating system, and is layered on top of Transarc's Encina 
Toolkit and the DCE to make distributed transactions reliable. 

     Version 2.0 of ACMSxp includes expanded distributed computing
functionality, making DCE- based applications easier to develop in
business-critical environments.  The remote procedure call has been 
enhanced to provide for the reliable operations of applications that 
must update data across multiples nodes in a network.  New task and 
record queuing features provide distributed, asynchronous 
communication between applications, making it possible to support 
deferred, batch and store and forward processing of requests.  In
addition, version 2.0 now adds Macintosh support to the growing list 
of client systems able to access ACMSxp server applications. 

     "ACMSxp and Encina complement each other to make our UNIX 
platforms especially attractive to customers who want to base their 
client/server applications on DCE," said John O'Keefe, Digital's 
vice president, UNIX Systems Group. "The combination of DCE and Encina 
Core Services provide a reliable set of distributed TP services that 
support ACMSxp.  This will facilitate the porting of the product to 
other platforms," he said. 

     ACMSxp is standards-based middleware for DEC OSF/1 UNIX 
platforms that provides an easy to use and flexible development 
environment for business-critical, client/server applications that 
meet the needs of customers' growing businesses.  ACMSxp offers a 
high-level, TP-specific programming language and a modular program 
structure for more rapid application development and a runtime 
environment that ensures high availability and performance. 

     "We are excited to add Digital to the long list of leaders in 
the open systems arena who have embraced DCE and Encina for their 
transaction processing strategies," said Alfred Spector, CEO and 
President of Transarc. "We are now delivering on the promise made 
less than a year ago to a majority of our current and prospective 
customers who demand Encina solutions on the Digital Alpha platform. 
We are thrilled that three open TP offerings on the platform are 
based on Encina technology." 

   ACMSxp V2.0 is priced from $100 to $200 for the client runtime 
version, and from $9,000 to $85,000 for the developers' version.  
The product will ship in January. 

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
